L81: `os.``environ`cite44†¶ L82: A cite45†mapping object where keys and values are strings that represent the process environment. For example, `environ['HOME']` is the pathname of your home directory (on some platforms), and is equivalent to `getenv("HOME")` in C.
L83: This mapping is captured the first time the cite0†`os` module is imported, typically during Python startup as part of processing `site.py`. Changes to the environment made after this time are not reflected in cite44†`os.environ` , except for changes made by modifying cite44†`os.environ` directly.
L84: 
L85: This mapping may be used to modify the environment as well as query the environment. cite46†`putenv()` will be called automatically when the mapping is modified.
L86: On Unix, keys and values use cite15†`sys.getfilesystemencoding()` and `'surrogateescape'` error handler. Use cite47†`environb` if you would like to use a different encoding.
L87: 
L88: On Windows, the keys are converted to uppercase. This also applies when getting, setting, or deleting an item. For example, `environ['monty'] = 'python'` maps the key `'MONTY'` to the value `'python'`.
L89: 
L90: Note
L91: 
L92: Calling cite46†`putenv()` directly does not change cite44†`os.environ` , so it’s better to modify cite44†`os.environ` .
L93: 
L94: Note
L95: On some platforms, including FreeBSD and macOS, setting `environ` may cause memory leaks. Refer to the system documentation for `putenv()`.
L96: 
L97: You can delete items in this mapping to unset environment variables. cite48†`unsetenv()` will be called automatically when an item is deleted from cite44†`os.environ` , and when one of the `pop()` or `clear()` methods is called.
